## Design: DB-level triggers, actor passed through the transaction

**Why triggers over app-level logging calls:** if you log at the call site (inside accounts.js/orders.js), every future route author has to remember to add a log line, and nothing stops a hard-to-audit path (a script, a psql session, a future route that calls `db.write` without logging) from writing unaudited. A trigger on the table fires no matter how the row changed, so coverage is structural, not a convention people can forget.

**The one gap triggers have:** they can't see your application's `req.user` — the DB connection is a shared pool credential, not a per-user login. Fix: since `db.write()` already opens exactly one transaction per call (db.js:16-18), have it set a transaction-scoped Postgres session variable with the actor id before running the real query. Triggers read that variable.

### 1. Audit table + generic trigger (schema/migration, not app code)

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name  text        NOT NULL,
  row_id      text        NOT NULL,
  action      text        NOT NULL CHECK (action IN ('INSERT','UPDATE','DELETE')),
  actor_id    uuid,
  changed_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  old_row     jsonb,
